II. Insertion Sort
Definition
Insertion Sort is a simple comparison-based sorting algorithm that builds the sorted array one element at a time.
It works by taking each element and inserting it into its correct position among the previously sorted elements.
The algorithm starts from the second element and compares it with the elements before it. If the previous elements are greater,
they are shifted one position to the right until the correct position for the current element is found.

Time Complexity
Best Case: O(n)
Average Case: O(n²)
Worst Case: O(n²)
Auxiliary Space
Insertion Sort requires O(1) auxiliary space because it sorts the elements in-place and uses only a small number of
additional variables such as the key and index variables.
Conclusion
Insertion Sort is a simple and efficient algorithm for sorting small or nearly sorted arrays.
It is easy to implement and requires only O(1) auxiliary space. Although its average and worst-case time complexity is O(n²), 
it performs very well when the input array is already or almost sorted. Therefore, Insertion Sort is useful for small datasets and 
situations where simplicity and low memory usage are important.

